There is no safe level1 of lead.  CleanEarth4Kids.org calls for the immediate stop of the sale, storage
and use of leaded fuel without exception.
Lead is a dangerous neurotoxin that accumulates over time.  There is no safe level of exposure to
lead according to the World Health Organization2, Centers for Disease Control3 and the American
Academy of Pediatrics.4
Lead is especially toxic5 to children and unborn babies. It damages kids' brains and nervous
systems, lowers IQ, causes behavior problems and is linked to higher rates of suspension and
detention6 along with lower reading and math test scores.7 According to the EPA8, lead does not
break down in the environment and is toxic even at very low exposure levels.
From the FAA9, leaded aviation fuel is used in almost 170,000 piston-engine planes & helicopters
(general aviation) in over 20,000 airports in the US.  Less than 1/4% of Americans are pilots for
these aircraft.  These planes and helicopters cause 70% of the lead pollution10 in our air!  Over 5
million people, including over 360,000 children11 under the age of 5, live near at least one airport
where these aircraft fly.
Multiple studies12 have shown that children who live near these airports have higher levels of lead
in their blood and most high lead emission airports are in communities of color.
Lead poisoning disproportionately affects communities of color13, with black children having the
highest concentration of lead in their blood. Lead in aviation fuel is an environmental, racial and
social justice issue.
The EPA and FAA have known this lead poisoning has been going on for decades…the EPA can not
delay any longer.  Lead is clearly a threat to human health!
