Under the “Proposed Rule”, the EPA claims authority under section 231(a)(2)(A) of the Clean Air Act to both “find that 
lead air pollution may reasonably be anticipated to endanger the public health and welfare” and additionally “to find 
that engine emissions of lead from certain aircraft cause or contribute to the lead air pollution that may reasonable be 
anticipated to endanger public health and welfare”. 
 
The fact is that leaded fuels used in Avgas today is substantially lower in harmful emissions now than what many of us 
remember in older automobiles and all but a very small amount of lead emissions has been eliminated. The AvGas of 
today accounts for approximately 470 tons/year of lead emissions, or less than ¼ of 1% of the lead emissions of the 70’s. 
 
