National Center for Healthy Housing Response to EPA’s Request for Public Comment on Lead 
Emissions from Aircraft Engines  
Docket EPA–HQ–OAR– 2022–0389 (Submitted electronically via: http://www.regulations.gov) 
 
To whom it may concern:  
The National Center for Healthy Housing (NCHH) applauds this move by the Environmental Protection 
Agency (EPA) toward an endangerment finding and appreciates the opportunity to comment on the 
proposed finding.  
NCHH is the preeminent national nonprofit dedicated to securing healthy homes for all. Since 1992, 
NCHH has served as a highly regarded and credible change agent, successfully integrating healthy 
housing advocacy, research, and capacity building under one roof to reduce health disparities 
nationwide. We have completed numerous scientific research and policy projects on the lasting impact 
of lead hazards in paint, dust, soil, water, and the air and the need to address and eliminate these 
sources of lead. 
There is no safe level of lead exposure for children, and an estimated 590,000 children in America have 
blood lead levels above the CDC’s reference level. Lead exposure causes irreversible tissue damage 
during childhood development and is responsible for nearly half a million adults dying annually from 
cardiovascular disease. Lead exposure is also an urgent environmental and racial justice issue: Black 
children are more likely to be exposed to lead and suffer its harmful effects than White and Hispanic 
children due to decades of redlining and other racist housing policies. 
It is critical that federal agencies, including EPA, take a holistic approach to protect children and 
communities from lead exposure. This approach must include regulating aviation gas, which accounts 
for 70 percent of lead emissions into the nation’s air; approximately 167,000 piston engine aircraft emit 
about 450 tons each year. Lead particles from aviation gas contaminate air and settle in the 
environment, including in the soil. EPA estimates that over 5 million people, including 136,000 children, 
live or attend school within 500 meters of an airport runway. That is equivalent to roughly 6,476 
elementary school classrooms full of children needlessly exposed to lead. Urban areas with larger 
amounts of traffic by piston-engine aircraft are especially at risk from this avenue of lead exposure.  
One study found that children who lived within six-tenths of a mile from an airport had blood lead levels 
that were 5.7 percent higher than blood lead levels of children who lived more than 2.5 miles from 
airports. Using these data, the 10 Policies to Prevent and Respond to Childhood Lead Exposure report 
released by the Health Impact Project in 2017 found that removing lead from aviation gas would protect 
226,000 children born in 2018 and provide at least $262 million in positive benefits.  

10320 Little Patuxent Parkway, Suite 200    Columbia, MD 21044 
410.992.0712 (p)    443.539.4150 (f) 
www.nchh.org    @NCHH    facebook.com/HealthyHousing 
 
The nation acted to ban lead in residential paint in 1978, most lead additives from gasoline in motor 
vehicles in 1986, and lead service lines in 1986. While these actions mean that we have made great 
progress in lowering blood lead levels in this country over the past several decades, the toxic legacy of 
lead lives on in our homes and environment and the continued use of leaded gasoline in piston-engine 
aircraft contributes to this problem.  
EPA must act to ban leaded aviation gasoline as part of a holistic solution to finish the fight against 
childhood lead poisoning. We strongly recommend EPA finalize the proposed endangerment finding as 
soon as possible and work with the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) to phase out lead aviation 
gasoline. To that end, we also support the language in the recently adopted Fiscal Year 2023 Omnibus, 
which directs the FAA to prioritize the identification and testing of unleaded replacement fuels that are 
viable and to move forward expeditiously on a rulemaking triggered by EPA’s endangerment finding."
