Science & Technology (EST) | American Chemical Society (ACS). Ethylene dibromide (EDB) and 1,2 
dichloroethane (EDC) persist at high levels despite a phaseout in the late 1980s, but they get little 
attention. As of the early 2000s, only 11 states require testing for Ethylene Dibromide (EDB) in 
groundwater at sites contaminated by leaded gasoline including 100LL Leaded Aviation Fuel with 
Tetraethyl Lead (TEL) that contains equal amounts of Ethylene dibromide (EDB) as a chemical scavenger. 
