I am writing to express my support for the findings that lead emissions from aircraft 
engines are dangerous for society, and I hope to see that these findings are approved. I believe 
there are many merits to this decision, and, while I know this proposal would not directly 
regulate aircraft lead emissions, I believe that it is necessary to take the steps toward lead 
regulation.
I am a graduate student at the Pratt Institute, pursuing a Master of Science in Urban and 
Community Planning. The stated purpose of the program on the school’s website is to give 
“students perspectives and skills to plan and implement equity-building improvements for 
neighborhoods, cities, and metropolitan areas.”  These are goals that I hope to achieve in my 
1
work when I become a planner. I want to see a better future for all, and I recognize that, in order 
to achieve this future, we need to use equitable means. In her work “The Minority-Race Planner 
in the Quest for a Just City,” June Manning Thomas explores this idea in more depth, explaining 
that, if in our pursuit for a just city, we end up using inequitable means, then that just city is a 
failure.  Thus, as someone who wants to be able to realize a just city, I know that we must 
2
practice equity while we work to achieve it. I believe that one way to accomplish an equitable 
future is through the regulation of lead pollution.
This proposed rule, which will find that lead emissions from specific aircraft engines are 
dangerous to public health and welfare, is a strong first step to reaching a just city and an 
