(2017)). 
20 Miranda et al., supra n.5, at 1513. 

 
 
 
4 
among children sampled during a downturn in piston-engine aircraft traffic, and increased 
dose-responsively in the flow of piston-engine aircraft traffic across a subset of airports.21 We 
are not aware of any study contradicting the conclusion that, for children living near general 
aviation airports, piston-engine aircraft traffic is correlated with an increase in blood lead 
levels. 
 
 
According to EPA, 4 million people reside—and about 600 K-12 schools are 
located—within 500 meters of airport runways used by piston engine aircraft.22 The 
exposure of these communities to daily, uncontrolled sources of lead contamination is 
unacceptable. EPA must act, and must do so quickly. 
 
III. 
Low-income communities and communities of color are disproportionately 
burdened by lead air pollution 
 
 
In the preface to EPA’s Strategy to Reduce Lead Exposures and Disparities in U.S. 
Communities, EPA Administrator Regan wrote: 
 
 
Although naturally occurring, lead is undoubtedly one of society’s most 
pervasive environmental toxins. Lead exposure can have devastating impacts 
to human health and can be especially harmful to developing children. We also 
know that because of existing racial and socioeconomic disparities, communities 
that have been historically marginalized and overburdened suffer the most. . . .23 
 
It is evident that airborne lead emissions from piston-engine aircraft contribute to this 
toxic lead exposure, and that EPA must take urgent action to address this public health 
crisis facing children across the country—and particularly children living in environmental 
justice communities.  
 
 
“The risk for lead exposure is not the same for all children.”24 Multiple studies show 
that Black children and children from low-income households “have a statistically 
significant increased risk of higher [blood lead levels]” compared to non-Hispanic white 
children and children from higher income households.25 Their exposure to lead pollution is 
cumulative. Low-income communities and communities of color are more likely to live near 
 
