We submit these comments on behalf of the Natural Resources Defense Council, 
Inc., American Bottom Conservancy, Clean Air Council, Clean Air Watch, Kentucky 
Resources Council, Learning Disabilities Association of America, Missouri Coalition for 
the Environment, Science and Environmental Health Network, Trust for Lead Poisoning 
Prevention, and the Wasatch Clean Air Coalition. 
 
EPA has sufficient basis and evidence to make an endangerment finding. The 
ANPR presents overwhelming evidence showing that the use of leaded aviation gasoline 
(“avgas”) causes or contributes to air pollution which may reasonably be anticipated to 
endanger public health or welfare. Therefore, EPA should promulgate emission standards 
for lead air pollution from aircraft engines.  
 
I. 
The Evidence Supports an Endangerment Finding 
 
 
Pursuant to the Clean Air Act, 42 U.S.C. § 7571, the endangerment finding 
which determines the need for regulation consists of two inquiries: 1) whether pollution 
from a class or classes of aircraft engines “causes or contributes” to air pollution, and 2) 
whether that air pollution “may reasonably be anticipated to endanger public health and 
welfare.”  The evidence supports an affirmative answer to both inquiries.  
 
A. 
Leaded Avgas “Causes or Contributes” to Lead Air Pollution 
 
 
To trigger regulation, the Clean Air Act merely requires a contribution from a 
class or classes of aircraft engines to the air pollution problem in question.  42 U.S.C. § 
7571.  In Bluewater Network v. EPA, 370 F.3d 1, 13-15 (D.C. Cir. 2004), the decision 
