interpreted a very similar provision addressing emissions from motor vehicle engines, 
and relied in part on the ordinary meaning of “contribute”—i.e. “to have a share in any 
act or effect”—and on contrasting requirements of a “significant” contribution 
elsewhere in the statute to hold that, in order to trigger regulation, the statute “does not 
require a finding of ‘significant contribution,’ but merely ‘contribution.’”  (Internal 
quotation omitted).  In Bluewater Network, the court found a 1.2% contribution to the 
carbon monoxide emissions inventory for a given area sufficient to justify regulation.  
Id. at 15.   
 
As EPA has documented amply, leaded avgas used in aircraft engines 
constitutes a major share of lead air pollution.  It thus easily meets the “cause or 
contribute” criterion.  The ANPR points to both a Swiss study of piston-engine aircraft 
emissions and an analysis of emissions from automobile engines using the same form of 
lead and lead scavenger to establish that lead is emitted from piston-engine aircraft 
using leaded avgas.  75 Fed. Reg. at 22456-57.  The ANPR also establishes that lead 
emitted by piston-engine aircraft using leaded avgas is, in aggregate, the largest source 
of lead to the air in the United States, contributing nearly half of the National Emissions 
Inventory in 2005 and constituting the single largest source category emitting lead to 
air.  75 Fed. Reg. at 22452, 22453.  EPA calculations show that aircraft in the US 
emitted approximately 779 short tons of lead, based on FAA estimates of piston engine 
activity annually.1  Id. at 22452-53.  These estimates are likely underestimates; as the 
EPA recognizes, leaded avgas comes in two forms—100LL (up to 2.12 grams of 
lead/gallon) and 100 Octane (up to 4.24 grams of lead/gallon)—and EPA has based its 
calculations exclusively on the less lead-intensive 100LL, which constitutes the 
dominant, but not the sole, use.  Id. at 22454.  Although lead emissions from a single 
aircraft are spread over the course of the flight path, the high frequency of aircraft 
activity surrounding airports results in a concentration of these emissions that can result 
in elevated levels of lead in the air and, through deposition, in soils. Id. at 22457-58. 
The ANPR establishes without a doubt that leaded avgas “contributes” to lead air 
pollution, particularly around airports. 
 
B. 
Lead Air Pollution May Reasonably Be Anticipated to Endanger 
Public Health and Welfare 
 
 
The phrase “which may reasonably be anticipated to endanger public health and 
welfare” is intended to require regulation “‘even if the regulator is less than sure that 
harm is otherwise inevitable.’”  Massachusetts v. EPA, 549 U.S. 497, 506 (2007) 
(addressing the same phrase in 42 U.S.C. § 7521(a)(1) and noting that the amendment 
to add that phrase to the Clean Air Act codified the decision in Ethyl Corp. v. EPA, 541 
F.2d 1, 25 (D.C. Cir. 1976), the holding of which included the precautionary quoted 
statement).  The health risks associated with lead easily satisfy this standard.   
                                                 
1 While EPA states that it will rely on the FAA fuel volume consumption data in 
developing the draft 2008 National Emission Inventory, the figure on the same page 
relies on the DOE data.  Id. at 22453.  This discrepancy is confusing and this data 
should more appropriately be presented using the FAA data. 
 

