Lead’s health effects are well established and long-recognized by both EPA and 
the courts.  See, e.g., 75 Fed. Reg. at 22444 (listing of lead as criteria pollutant in 1976); 
Ethyl Corp. v. EPA, 541 F.2d 1, 25 (D.C. Cir. 1976).  Lead emitted by piston-engine 
aircraft are in the size range retained and absorbed by humans and animals.  75 Fed. 
Reg. at 22457.  Lead emitted by aircraft may deposit locally at times of landing and 
takeoff and may also be more broadly dispersed during flight, although lead emissions 
and levels are greatest near airports where leaded avgas is used.  Id. at 22442, 22458.  
As NRDC commented during the lead NAAQS rulemaking, and as the lead NAAQS 
final rule established, there is no known safe level of lead.  Id. at 22448, 22449.  Lead is 
associated with a broad array of harmful effects in both adults and children, and 
environmental justice populations face disproportionately greater risks from lead, as 
EPA has previously detailed in both the lead NAAQS rulemaking documents and in the 
ANPR here.  Id. at 22445, 22449-50.  Lead’s health effects include impairment of 
kidney, cardiovascular, and immune function; and interference with neurological 
development and function, with manifestations including sensory, motor, cognitive and 
behavioral impacts, as well as decrements in IQ scores and attention and learning 
functions.  Id. at 22449-50 (citing lead air quality criteria document relied upon in the 
development of the lead national ambient air quality standards).  Lead has also been 
identified as a probable human carcinogen by EPA and the International Agency for 
Research on Cancer, and the cancer risks of lead have been recognized by several other 
respected organizations.  Id. at 22449.  Lead bioaccumulates in the body, id., and thus 
additive exposures are of particular concern.  Children are particularly sensitive to the 
health effects of lead.  Id.   
 
The studies evaluated by EPA suggest that lead emissions and levels at airports 
where leaded avgas is used are greatest near the airport.  See id. at 22548.  The 
concentration of lead emissions from piston engine aircraft near airports results in 
exposures that can endanger the health of nearby populations. Routes of exposure 
include inhalation of airborne lead as well as ingestion of lead through contact with 
surfaces onto which lead has been deposited or tracked such as soil, plants, floors, 
furniture, and toys.  Id. at 22460.  EPA’s current estimates indicate that over 3 million 
children attend schools within one kilometer of the centroid of an airport where leaded 
avgas is used, and that these schools have a disproportionate representation of lower-
income students and African-American or Hispanic students.  Id. at 22461.  African 
American, Hispanic and low-income children have higher rates of elevated blood lead 
levels due to higher rates of exposures to lead pollution.2 EPA also estimates that 16 
million people live within one kilometer of the centroid of airports where leaded avgas 
is used and that incomes are likely to be lower in these areas.  Id.  The actual number of 
people exposed is likely even higher than EPA estimates.  We believe that the 
appropriate analysis of exposure should measure exposure from the perimeter and not 
                                                 
2 Levin,R. Brown, MJ, Kashtock, ME,  Jacobs, DE, Whelan, EA, Rodman, J, 
Schock, MR, Padilla, A and Sinks, T. 2008. Lead Exposures in U.S. Children, 2008: 
Implications for Prevention. Environmental Health Perspectives 116: 10. (Attached as 
Exhibit A) 
 

Attn: Docket ID No. EPA-HQ-OAR-2007-0294 
August 27, 2010 
Page 4 of 6 
 
the center of the airport3 and due to wind and meteorological patterns could extend 
beyond one kilometer.  The use of average piston engine aircraft activity and 
characteristics to estimate emissions levels may underestimate exposures at individual 
airports. 
 
Other populations that work near or with piston engine-aircraft on a regular 
basis such as those engaged in agricultural activities and pilots, student-trainees, and 
passengers may also be exposed to higher levels of lead.  Id. at 22462.  In addition, lead 
deposited on crops during agricultural activities conducted with piston-engine aircraft 
may result in exposure to lead for the consumer of the crops.  Id.   
 
The ANPR clearly establishes that lead is associated with a range of health 
effects and that people, especially sensitive sub-populations, are likely to be exposed to 
lead emitted by aircraft.  Thus, lead may reasonably be anticipated to endanger people 
living in and around airports and elsewhere where lead is deposited, and EPA should 
propose regulations to establish emissions standards for lead emitted by aircraft using 
leaded avgas. 
 
 
  II. 
If Avgas Causes or Contributes to Lead Air Pollution and if Such Air 
Pollution May Reasonably Be Anticipated to Endanger Public Health or 
Welfare, EPA Must Propose Emission Standards.  
 
When the endangerment criteria are met, considerations about costs or hardship 
have no role to play in the activation of the obligation to issue proposed emission 
standards. The Clean Air Act (“CAA”) provides that the Administrator “shall . . . issue 
proposed emission standards applicable to the emission of any air pollutant from any 
class or classes of aircraft engines which in his judgment causes, or contributes to, air 
pollution which may reasonably be anticipated to endanger public health or welfare.”  
42 U.S.C. § 7571 (emphasis added).  The EPA must therefore issue proposed emission 
standards if the two criteria are met: 1) if the emission of an air pollutant from aircraft 
engines causes or contributes to air pollution, and 2) if that air pollution may reasonably 
be anticipated to endanger public health or welfare.  Because proposed regulations must 
issue when these criteria are met, other considerations may play no role in activating 
this obligation.  See Massachusetts v. EPA, 549 U.S. 497, 533 (2007) (interpreting 
virtually identical standard in the context of automobile emissions, 45 U.S.C. § 
7521(a)(1), and stating that “[i]f EPA makes a finding of endangerment, the Clean Air 
Act requires the agency to regulate emissions of the deleterious pollutant from new 
motor vehicles” and that “once EPA has responded to a petition for rulemaking, its 
reasons for action or inaction must conform to the authorizing statute”). Thus, concerns 
                                                 
3 Due to the size and layout of an airport the center or centroid may not accurately 
describe the location of maximum emissions.  This location is more accurately 
represented by the perimeter of the airport, which would ensure that the runways are 
fully captured, and this metric should be used to estimate the location and size of 
impacted populations. 
 


