Sammy Zahran of Mountain Data Group entitled Leaded Aviation Gasoline Exposure Risk at Reid-
Hillview Airport in Santa Clara County, California (“RHV Lead Study”).  A true and correct copy 
of the RHV Lead Study is attached hereto as Exhibit C. 
14. Among its results, the Lead Study found that children residing within a half-mile of the 
airport have blood lead levels that are an average of 0.2 µg/dL higher than those of statistically 
similar children more distant from the airport.  Children living downwind of the airport were at the 
greatest risk, with blood lead levels that were, on average, 0.4 µg/dL higher than their peers.  These 
children were also 200% more likely than children residing upwind of RHV to have blood lead 
levels above 4.5 µg/dL – the action threshold used by CDPH when testing for elevated blood lead.  
Measures of blood lead in local children also tracked changes in leaded avgas sale at the airport.  A 
change in the quantity of aviation gasoline sold from the observed minimum to the maximum was 
associated with an increase in child blood lead levels by about 0.18 μg/dL.   
15. 
In response to the Lead Study, the Board directed the County Administration to 
“continue working on securing unleaded aviation gasoline for the County Airports System” and to 
“take all actions necessary to transition to carrying only lead free gas at both County airports as soon 
as possible.”  A true and correct copy of the board report and minutes for the relevant item from the 
Board’s August 17, 2021 meeting are attached as Exhibits D and E, respectively.   
Avgas Availability in the U.S. 
16. 
Currently the most common aviation fuel (“avgas”) commercially available is a 
leaded 100 octane fuel known as 100LL.   
17. 
There are no commercially available unleaded avgas rated at 100 octane.  The FAA 
recently approved G100UL, a 100 octane unleaded avgas, for use in virtually all aircraft in the U.S. 
piston engine fleet, though G100UL is not yet commercially available. 
18. 
There is one commercially available 94 octane unleaded avgas, known as UL94, that 
is usable by a majority of the aircraft in the U.S. piston engine fleet.  Prior to January 1, 2022, only 
one airport west of the Rocky Mountains sold UL94.   
19. 
A minority of the aircraft in the U.S. piston engine fleet – typically aircraft with high 
performance engines – require a 100 octane avgas to operate.   
