e. Since July 2022, County officials have been in approximately weekly discussions 
with FAA regional officials regarding resolution of issues raised in the Part 13 
Notice. 
63. 
Since March 2022, the FAA and the County have exchanged six drafts of an MOU to 
resolve the issues noted in the Part 13 Notice.  The draft MOU includes the County’s participation in 
a pilot demonstration initiative relating to the sale of fuel at the County Airports.  The County sent 
its most recent draft of the MOU to the FAA on December 23, 2022.   
64. 
As part of its most recent draft of the MOU, the FAA indicated to the County that it 
was no longer able to discuss matters that were the subject of the Complaint as part of the MOU 
negotiations.  The pending adjudication of the Complaint is also inhibiting County staff from freely 
exchanging ideas with the FAA regarding fueling at the County Airports, which is crucial to our 
efforts to resolve the disputed issues. 
65. 
The FAA provided the County with multiple extensions to provide material 
responsive to the FAA’s February 22, 2022 Request for Additional Information.  The County 
provided additional information on February 24, 2022, May 9, 2022, and September 13, 2022.  The 
County’s efforts to respond to the request information are ongoing.  The County and the FAA are 
discussing incorporating the provision of the outstanding information into the MOU.   
66. 
The County responded to the letter December 13, 2021 from Aperture Aviation, Inc. 
on December 19, 2022 informing Aperture Aviation, Inc. that the County had not prohibited the use 
of leaded avgas at either County Airport.  The County has taken no action to prohibit Aperture 
Aviation, Inc. from using leaded avgas at the County Airports.  A true and correct copy of the 
County’s response is attached as Exhibit L. 
